Yes, you can achieve this in Kameleoon using the Widget Studio or by custom JavaScript in your experiment or personalization. Here’s how you can do it:

Option 1: Using Widget Studio (No-Code)
- Create your first popup (e.g., Newsletter Subscription) as a widget.
- Add a button to this popup (e.g., “Subscribe”).
- In the button’s action settings, set it to “Close this widget” and then “Open another widget” (your Thank You or Confirmation popup).
- Create the second popup (Thank You/Confirmation) as a separate widget.
- Link the button action from the first widget to open the second widget.

Option 2: Using Custom JavaScript (Advanced)
If you’re using custom popups or want more control, you can use JavaScript to listen for the button click and trigger the second popup. For example:

```javascript
// Assuming #subscribeBtn is the button in your first popup
document.getElementById('subscribeBtn').addEventListener('click', function() {
    // Close the first popup
    document.getElementById('newsletterPopup').style.display = 'none';
    // Open the thank you popup
    document.getElementById('thankYouPopup').style.display = 'block';
});
```

You can adapt this to your popup/widget IDs or classes.

Summary:
- With Widget Studio, you can chain popups using built-in actions.
- With custom code, you can control popup behavior programmatically.

If you need step-by-step instructions for Widget Studio or want a code example tailored to your setup, let me know which method you prefer!

